Closure-122.status_reason:
builder UNBUILDABLE: The C10 recipe cannot be carried validly by IRFactory.transformTree, for three independent reasons.

(1) No chain can be built. Each step of transformTree uses private instance state of a package-private class:
- the private constructor;
- the private transform(), which runs the private TransformDispatcher;
- the private parsedComments set;
- the private handlePossibleFileOverviewJsDoc and handleBlockComment;
- the private setFileOverviewJsDoc and the rootNodeJsDocHolder and fileOverviewInfo fields.
The facade body must become one delegating call to a public static hop in another class. The drawn hosts are in other packages and cannot see IRFactory, so the recipe's host rule makes them unusable. Only one usable pool host (JsDocInfoParser) shares the package. Making the chain compile would mean making the class public and exposing about eight private members as public API, called from five unrelated packages. That is a re-architecture of the real-bug class, not a small behaviour-preserving pre-edit like B3's guard rewrite. It would also put undeclared, newly public IRFactory methods on the trigger path as alternative owners. Moving the original body into a new IRFactory method would make that method the real owner.

(2) transformTree has no javadoc. The lie therefore has no documentation voice to join; rewrite_facade silently appends nothing, and the facade would need a newly authored javadoc.

(3) There is no single-test fault. transformTree runs on every parse in the suite, so only its rare paths can carry a fault:
- The suspicious-block-comment check is covered by six overlapping tests. No one-line omission in it, or in an equivalent hand-written scanner, is caught by exactly one test. Each omission is caught by 2, 4 or 5 tests, or by none.
- Omissions on the file-overview and license paths are caught by 0, 4, 6 or 10 tests (full-suite probes C1-C6).
Gate G5 (only the trigger newly fails) therefore cannot pass with a one-line omission.

Closure-37.status_reason:
builder UNBUILDABLE: The recipe cannot be applied to NodeTraversal.traverseRoots without breaking a validity rule. Two of the brief's own UNBUILDABLE examples apply: the computation cannot be split into a chain, and no fault is caught by exactly one test.

(1) The computation cannot be split into a chain. Both static overloads have a 2-line body: `new NodeTraversal(compiler, cb)` followed by `t.traverseRoots(roots)`. All of the actual work happens in the instance method traverseRoots(List) and in private machinery:
- it returns early on an empty list;
- it sets scopeRoot to roots.get(0).getParent(), with a checkState;
- it sets inputId, sourceName and curNode (all private fields);
- it calls pushScope(scopeRoot), which is private and pushes a lazily created scope root;
- for each root it runs checkState(root.getParent() == scopeRoot) and the private recursive traverseBranch;
- it calls the private popScope;
- it catches Exception and calls the private throwUnexpectedException.

The per-root core (the root loop that drives traverseBranch under one shared lazy scope) cannot move into a public static method of another class. The non-private entry points are traverseInnerNode, traverseWithScope, traverseAtScope and the per-call public traverseRoots. Each of these either traverses the parent node itself, or pushes an eagerly built Scope and calls enterScope/exitScope once per root. So none is equivalent for ScopedCallbacks. Exact equivalence would need a pre-edit that makes at least six private NodeTraversal members visible. That is far beyond the behaviour-preserving pre-edits the brief allows ('as in B3's guard rewrite').

(2) Even with such a pre-edit, no one-line omission in the resulting gold fails exactly one existing test (gate G5). I checked the gold line by line.

- **List overload.** It is a hub. In production it is called by VarCheck, Normalize.VerifyConstants, ReferenceCollectingCallback (all the inlining passes), SymbolTable (4 sites), TypedScopeCreator, MethodCompilerPass, StrictModeCheck and the MakeDeclaredNamesUnique test. The omissions split into two groups:
  - Omitting the empty-list return, either Preconditions check, the inputId reset or the sourceName reset changes nothing on any test input. Every caller passes [externs, root], whose shared parent is the synthetic externs+js BLOCK. NodeUtil.getInputId returns null for that BLOCK, and "" equals the constructor's default for sourceName.
  - Omitting curNode, pushScope or the per-root traversal makes every traversal through the facade throw or visit nothing. Omitting popScope skips the global exitScope, where ReferenceCollectingCallback updates the global references and runs afterExitScope, which breaks global inlining across many tests.
- **Varargs overload.** Its only caller is RemoveUnusedClassProperties.process. That pass runs in RemoveUnusedClassPropertiesTest (13 methods) and in every ADVANCED-mode compile, because removeUnusedPrototypeProperties also enables it. The callback is a plain Callback that ignores the NodeTraversal argument, so scope, inputId, sourceName, curNode and the exception wrapping are observed by no test through this path. The only behaviour exactly one test depends on is visiting the externs root. RemoveUnusedClassPropertiesTest::testExtern needs EXT.ext from the externs to pin this.ext; every other method of that class pins through main-code reads. But the root loop treats all roots alike. No omission drops only the first root. A gold that handled the first root separately would differ in structure from the original computation, and its omission would leave a visible range-start gap (checklist T3(b)). A ternary or branch that distinguishes externs would be a dead decision in the correct gold (T2(c)). Such a design could also be compensated at a hop in one line, for example by a hop traversing roots[0] itself, so the gold would not be the single owner.

So no valid trigger, single owner or no-local-trace gold exists for this facade. It is the instance's only allowed target, so per BATCH4_PREREGISTRATION the instance is UNBUILDABLE.

JacksonCore-20.status_reason:
builder UNBUILDABLE: collectDefaults is a zero-argument, uniform fold over the enum: one predicate (enabledByDefault) and one OR accumulation. Its only omissions change every enabled feature or every disabled feature at once, so they fail 60-76 tests and break G5; the empty-accumulation case is also a T2 frozen-local trace. A fault that only one test catches would have to remove exactly one feature, which needs a step tied to that feature or position. The original computation has no such step, and neither do JsonFactory.Feature.collectDefaults, JsonParser.Feature.collectDefaults or any other production code, so it would have no external pin. Every form considered either leaves a checklist trace or fails no test:
- naming one feature is T3(a)/(d);
- seeding the fold from the first feature with the loop starting at begin + 1 leaves, after the omission, a loop that visibly skips the range start (T3(b)/T4);
- a run-commit or Horner-style fold has no effect on the real enum order or collapses every bit (T2);
- an ordinal-fits-in-int guard is never exercised.

Because the facade takes no input, a one-line OR of a constant mask at any hop would also restore a lost bit, so the gold could not be the unique owner. collectDefaults is the instance's only allowed target, so the instance is UNBUILDABLE.
